===== /candidate/PROOF.md
     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	## What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied `MPY` semantics, for every mathematical integer `N` with
     6	`1 <= N <= 1000`, applying the exact translated
     7	`even_odd_palindrome` closure to `Int(N)` reaches a two-element tuple containing
     8	the number of even and odd positive decimal palindromes not exceeding `N`,
     9	respectively.
    10	
    11	This is a partial-correctness result in the Kit sense. The implementation has no
    12	loops or recursion: every call follows at most seven integer comparisons to a
    13	constant return.
    14	
    15	## Formal claim
    16	
    17	`spec.k` contains 108 disjoint reachability claims. There is one claim for each
    18	interval beginning at a palindrome and ending immediately before the next
    19	palindrome; the last interval is `999..1000`. Their union is exactly
    20	`1..1000`.
    21	
    22	Each claim starts with:
    23	
    24	```k
    25	<k> Call(solutionClosure(), Int(N)) => EXPECTED-TUPLE </k>
    26	```
    27	
    28	It also pins the initial and final environment, scope store, scope allocator,
    29	heap, heap allocator, call stack, return state, exception state, and exit code.
    30	The function call, parameter binding, comparisons, branches, tuple creation,
    31	return, and frame restoration all execute using the unchanged supplied
    32	semantics.
    33	
    34	The intended count table follows from the complete characterization, within the
    35	formal domain, of positive decimal palindromes:
    36	
    37	- `1..9`;
    38	- `11*a` for `1 <= a <= 9`;
    39	- `100*a + 10*b + a` for `1 <= a <= 9` and `0 <= b <= 9`.
    40	
    41	The parity of a two-digit palindrome is the parity of `a`; the parity of a
    42	three-digit palindrome is also the parity of `a`. `1000` is not a palindrome.
    43	Thus the cumulative pair is constant between successive palindrome thresholds,
    44	which is exactly the interval structure stated in `spec.k`.
    45	
    46	## Proof-extension inventory
    47	
    48	There is exactly one proof-local function/rule in `verification.k`.
    49	
    50	| Field | Record |
    51	|---|---|
    52	| Extension | `solutionClosure()` and its single defining rule |
    53	| Class | Definitional summary |
    54	| Semantic role | Names one exact closure value; it does not replace or accelerate any program execution |
    55	| Domain | The single nullary term `solutionClosure()`; `[total]` is justified by its one exhaustive equation |
    56	| Matched context | In every target claim it occurs only as the callee of `Call(solutionClosure(), Int(N))` in the fully pinned configuration shown in `spec.k` |
    57	| Justification scope | The exact parameters, statement tree, and definition location transliterated from `solution.py` into `solution.mpy` |
    58	| Context containment | The equation merely unfolds to that exact closure value; all call routing, parameter binding, body control, return, and frame restoration remain fixed-semantics steps |
    59	| State footprint | The equation itself reads and writes no cells; subsequent fixed execution uses the cells pinned by each claim |
    60	| Value influence | Selects the exact body whose constant tuple leaf becomes the result |
    61	| Value justification | Its exhaustive equation fully fixes the closure value; regeneration from `solution.mpy` and the rejected body mutation validate identity and sensitivity |
    62	| Justification | Mechanical extraction from the exact translator output; no opaque value or oracle occurs in execution |
    63	| Dependents | All 108 claims in `SPEC` |
    64	| Control validation | No operational bridge exists. LLVM smoke execution terminates with `.K`, `NoExc`, and exit code 0 |
    65	| Value validation | Translator identity check passes; changing the `N=1` body leaf from `(0,1)` to `(9,9)` makes the original result claim fail |
    66	| Validation | Gate A body mutation, false-result mutation, concrete smoke cases, exhaustive independent oracle, and direct actual-spec audit all pass |
    67	
    68	The 108 reachability claims are theorem statements, not rules used to bypass
    69	execution. There are no proof-local simplification rules, priority rules,
    70	operational bridges, trusted primitives, opaque symbols, or auxiliary
    71	circularities.
